WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.800
Security is affected not only by compiler flags, but also by runtime library and standard library configuration.

2
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:14.000
Debug runtimes, checked iterators, hardened library modes, bounds-related checks, and allocator diagnostics can expose assumptions that ordinary

3
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:16.000
release settings may not show.

4
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:24.000
Checked iterators and debug runtime modes can catch invalid iterator use, container misuse, mismatched allocation behavior, and other defects during

5
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:24.400
development.

6
00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:32.800
They are often too expensive or too behavior-changing for all production builds, but they provide valuable evidence in test profiles.

7
00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:41.200
Safer C++ abstractions reduce reliance on low-level manual memory handling. `std::span`, `std::string`, `std::vector`, smart pointers, RAII wrappers,

8
00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:46.000
and range-based operations can make ownership and bounds easier to review.

9
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:51.600
Library hardening works best when the code already uses abstractions that carry useful information.

10
00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:55.200
Build profiles should balance safety checks, performance, and compatibility.

11
00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:58.000
Development and sanitizer profiles can be stricter.

12
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:01.200
Staging can mirror production while adding selected diagnostics.

13
00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:06.800
Release profiles should be intentional and verified against risk, performance requirements, and platform support.

14
00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:10.000
The important habit is to document the tradeoff.

15
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:14.800
If a hardened library mode is enabled only in testing, say why.

16
00:01:14.800 --> 00:01:21.600
If a release build disables a check for performance or compatibility, record the rationale and compensating controls.

17
00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:24.400
Governance turns configuration into an engineering decision.
